John Kelley
Acting U.S. Alternate Representative
New York, New York
February 27, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And for their comprehensive briefings, I’d like to thank Under-Secretary-General Lacroix, UN Police Advisor Shahkar, MINUSCA Police Commissioner Christophe Bizimungu, and UNFICYP Senior Police Advisor Mingzhu Xu.
UN Police extensively contribute to peacekeeping by supporting Member States, where mandated, in conflict, post-conflict, and crisis situations through efficient engagement that serves and protects the population.
UN Police in peacekeeping operations are a cost-effective way for international burden-sharing and the UN Secretariat increasingly recognizes them as being central to the success of UN PKOs.
Controlling civil disorder, border-policing, training and mentoring host-country police, protecting civilians, and institutionalizing the rule of law are just some of the ways UN police contribute to missions.
As put forward in the latest Independent Study on the Future of Peacekeeping, peacekeeping must adapt. UNPOL must also adapt as a critical piece of the peacekeeping puzzle.
One helpful adaptation would be to enable more effective integration of UNPOL perspectives and equities in decision-making processes at both UN Headquarters and at the mission level.
This integration would strengthen strategic and operational mission planning conversations, which would have important planning and resourcing implications, including helping to ensure the recruitment of UNPOL personnel is centered more on mission-specific needs and skillsets.
Another helpful adaptation could be to extend UNPOL rotation schedules as part of an effort to strengthen some of its institutional structures at the Headquarters level.
Longer rotations would also support the development of critical local knowledge and relationships, key to the effective mentoring and capacity-building of local police in UN peacekeeping contexts.
This could also help address the issue of the demand for UN Police officers and Formed Police Units outpacing the number of police officers contributing states are willing and able to deploy.
In addition, training must match the growing complexity of policing. Member States must prepare police with specialized training, especially in critical areas such as conducting forensic investigations, including digital forensics, and addressing organized crime.
At the same time, it’s vital to leverage digital technologies to enhance UNPOL and broader peacekeeping effectiveness, improve mandate delivery, and analyze data to develop evidence-based approaches.
In closing, Mr. President, UNPOL continues to show the capacity and willingness to innovate to meet new and emerging challenges, and it must continue to uphold high standards of ethical conduct to be able to effectively bridge the gap between missions, host state law enforcement, and local communities.
Member States, missions, and the UN Secretariat should work together to meet UNPOL’s growing needs and rising expectations for its critical role in UN peacekeeping.
And with that I thank you, Mr. President.
