Statement Exchange of Information on State Practices on the Peaceful Settlement of Disputes:“Use of Procedures Envisaged in the Charter and Other International Instruments”
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair.
The United States welcomes the opportunity to provide observations on the peaceful settlement of disputes through the use of procedures envisaged in the Charter and other international instruments.
Article 33 of the UN Charter sets forth a non-exclusive list of pacific tools for dispute resolution, which we have previously had the opportunity to discuss in this committee. These include mediation, arbitration, and judicial settlement. Article 33 also provides that the Security Council may call on parties to settle their dispute by these means.
These forms of dispute resolution are found in numerous international instruments. These instruments reflect the dispute resolution procedures the parties to the agreements found most appropriate for resolving disagreements under that instrument. This may include reference to the International Court of Justice, in the case of the UN and its specialized agencies. It may mean resorting to arbitration, such as in the cases of investment disputes pursuant to various multilateral or bilateral investment treaties. Other instruments provide frameworks for states to negotiate disputes pertaining to particular issues, be it law enforcement, the protection of human rights, or global migration.
Crucial to all these means of dispute resolution is that the parties mutually consent to the means most suitable to them. This is emphasized by Article 36, paragraph 2, which expressly provides that the Security Council should take into consideration any dispute settlement procedures the parties to the dispute have already adopted. Paragraph 3 of the same Article makes clear that in making any recommendations in relation to disputes under Article 33, the Security Council should take into consideration that it is the parties themselves that as a general rule refer matters to the International Court of Justice.
From this we see that the peaceful settlement of disputes is most effective when it is driven by the parties themselves and undertaken under the modalities those parties have determined best serve their respective interests. The UN Charter appropriately highlights the importance of a party-led process. The many international instruments that contain dispute resolution clauses demonstrate the care with which parties to those instruments determine those
means that are most appropriate to resolving disputes as to particular issues. As much as possible, those decisions should be respected.
In conclusion, we appreciate the opportunity to hear from other members of the Charter Committee about their views on and experiences with the dispute resolution procedures envisaged in the Charter and other international instruments.
