Dorothy Patton
Attorney-Adviser
New York, New York
February 18, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair.
We welcome the opportunity to participate in the work of the Special Committee on the UN Charter as it sets out to examine and strengthen the UN Charter.
We look forward to the annual thematic debate on the peaceful settlement of disputes. The discussions in years past have proven valuable in reiterating the diverse means of resolving disputes. We believe this year’s focus on the use of procedures envisaged in the Charter and other international instruments, will prove equally fruitful.
With respect to the annual briefing on sanctions, we also look forward to discussing how the implementation of targeted sanctions adopted by the Security Council in accordance with the UN Charter can be enhanced. We look forward to discussions in the Informal Working Group on sanctions regarding how to ensure compliance. With respect to sanctions implemented outside of UN auspices, and appreciating that these are not the focus of this Committee’s work, we make clear that such sanctions can be a legal and legitimate means to achieve foreign policy, security, and other important objectives.
With respect to other topics under this agenda item, we regret to see several older proposals that are duplicative or inconsistent with the roles of the principal organs of the United Nations as set forth in the Charter. We hope this Committee can move on from these proposals, which include a legal study of General Assembly powers, proposals on UN reform, and requesting an advisory opinion from the International Court of Justice.
We also regret the continued inclusion of proposals that raise bilateral concerns that are outside the Special Committee’s mandate, or are more appropriately raised in other fora. In particular, we do not support the proposal concerning unilateral coercive measures, and we also believe that the proposal concerning Article 51 is better addressed in other fora.
We urge delegations to withdraw these and other proposals that have remained on the Special Committee’s agenda for years without generating consensus. The Special Committee should instead focus its attention on proposals that are practical, non-political, and not duplicative of efforts elsewhere in the United Nations. The Special Committee can be a forum
for meaningful discussion, but not if its agenda is dominated by politically charged topics for which our past sessions demonstrate there is little prospect of consensus. We hope the Special Committee will take further steps to improve its efficiency and productivity and to make the best use of scarce Secretariat resources. This includes holding shortened sessions or moving to biennial meetings.
In closing, the United States extends its thanks to the Secretariat of the Special Committee, the Bureau, support staff, and all those who contribute to the Committee’s work.
Thank you.
